WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.451
External input should be treated as untrusted until the program validates it.

2
00:00:04.451 --> 00:00:11.129
Input can arrive through files, sockets, command-line arguments, environment variables, IPC, configuration, serialized data, plugin interfaces, or

3
00:00:11.129 --> 00:00:11.871
user interfaces.

4
00:00:11.871 --> 00:00:14.838
Some of it may be malformed by accident.

5
00:00:14.838 --> 00:00:19.290
Some of it may reflect old versions, partial writes, or incompatible producers.

6
00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:22.257
Good parsers validate structure before relying on it.

7
00:00:22.257 --> 00:00:26.338
That includes lengths, counts, offsets, versions, flags, encodings, and state transitions.

8
00:00:26.338 --> 00:00:32.644
If a message says it contains ten records, the parser should confirm that ten records actually fit.

9
00:00:32.644 --> 00:00:40.063
If an offset points into a buffer, the parser should confirm that the pointed-to range exists before using it.

10
00:00:40.063 --> 00:00:42.289
Numeric conversion deserves its own care.

11
00:00:42.289 --> 00:00:46.741
Code should detect invalid input, range errors, overflow, underflow, and leftover characters.

12
00:00:46.741 --> 00:00:52.305
A partial parse can be dangerous when the program treats it as a clean value.

13
00:00:52.305 --> 00:00:58.982
Strong parsing returns both the converted value and a clear status about whether the entire input met expectations.

14
00:00:58.982 --> 00:01:03.434
Validation should occur before allocation, copying, indexing, dispatch, or state-changing operations.

15
00:01:03.434 --> 00:01:11.966
For example, a length from input should not be used to allocate a huge buffer before checking whether the length is within policy.

16
00:01:11.966 --> 00:01:17.901
A type field should not dispatch to a handler before the surrounding structure has been validated.

17
00:01:17.901 --> 00:01:21.611
Failure behavior should be explicit, safe, and easy to test.

18
00:01:21.611 --> 00:01:31.255
A parser can reject a record, return an error object, close a connection, skip an optional field, or enter a recovery path, but it should not

19
00:01:31.255 --> 00:01:33.852
continue with stale data or guessed defaults.

20
00:01:33.852 --> 00:01:38.304
Logs should explain what category of validation failed without exposing sensitive content.

21
00:01:38.304 --> 00:01:40.158
Secure parsing is repeatable engineering.

22
00:01:40.158 --> 00:01:45.352
Define the accepted format, validate before trust, represent failures clearly, and test boundary cases.

23
00:01:45.352 --> 00:01:52.400
Reviewers should ask which inputs are trusted, which are merely parsed, and which are allowed to change program state.
